WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.581
DEI is not only about who is in the team.

2
00:00:05.931 --> 00:00:08.150
It also shapes how research is designed.

3
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.756
It starts with the question itself.

4
00:00:11.106 --> 00:00:13.858
Whose problem is being studied, and who defined it?

5
00:00:14.208 --> 00:00:18.261
Involving affected communities early helps make the research relevant.

6
00:00:19.011 --> 00:00:22.963
Sampling and recruitment should make participant groups representative

7
00:00:22.963 --> 00:00:25.390
of the population affected by the research.

8
00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:32.135
Data collection should use accessible, culturally and linguistically appropriate instruments and settings.

9
00:00:32.885 --> 00:00:36.840
And analysis and sharing should report results for relevant groups

10
00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:40.735
where appropriate, and return findings to all participant groups.

11
00:00:41.485 --> 00:00:43.491
Bias can enter at every stage.

12
00:00:43.841 --> 00:00:47.851
In planning, questions may reflect only the team's own experience.

13
00:00:48.601 --> 00:00:54.169
In recruitment, narrow channels and strict criteria can exclude underrepresented groups.

14
00:00:54.919 --> 00:01:00.338
In measurement, tools validated in one population may be applied uncritically to others.

15
00:01:01.088 --> 00:01:05.163
And in interpretation, the majority group may be treated as the norm.

16
00:01:05.513 --> 00:01:09.659
Diverse teams, transparent criteria, bias training and regular

17
00:01:09.659 --> 00:01:12.467
monitoring all help to reduce these risks.

